\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Triangular factorization of a split map}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring and
#{M=X\oplus Y} a \vocabk{chosen binary splitting}{fdip-001T}
of unital #{R}-modules. Let #{Q(x,y)=(Ax+By,Ex+Cy)} have the
[typed components](fdip-001T) of this splitting, and assume
#{C:Y\to Y} is an isomorphism. Set
##{K=C^{-1}E:X\to Y,\qquad S=A-BC^{-1}E:X\to X.}
Define three endomorphisms of #{M} by
##{\begin{aligned}
U(x,y)&=(x+BC^{-1}y,y),\\
D(x,y)&=(Sx,Cy),\\
V(x,y)&=(x,y+Kx).
\end{aligned}}
Then #{U,V} are isomorphisms and, with right-factor-first composition,
##{Q=UDV.}}

\proof{
\p{The displayed maps are linear since every component is linear.
Direct substitution on both sides verifies the inverses
##{U^{-1}(x,y)=(x-BC^{-1}y,y),\qquad
   V^{-1}(x,y)=(x,y-Kx).}
These inverses require no inverse of #{S}. Applying the three factors
in order gives
##{\begin{aligned}
DV(x,y)&=(Sx,Ex+Cy),\\
UDV(x,y)&=(Sx+BC^{-1}(Ex+Cy),Ex+Cy)\\
        &=(Ax+By,Ex+Cy)=Q(x,y),
\end{aligned}}
where #{S+BC^{-1}E=A} and #{C^{-1}C=I_Y} give the last equality.}
}

\p{\citet{Appendix C.4.1, pp. 672-673}{boyd2004convex} gives real
block-system elimination. The proof above extends it directly to
modules. The source uses first-pivot order;
[swapping to the eliminated summand](fdip-001T) gives the
convention here. No finite rank, projectivity or determinant is used.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{CategoryTheory.Biprod.gaussian'}
supports elimination in a preadditive category with binary biproducts
and an invertible first component. In the order #{(Y,X)}, use
#{(C,B,E,A)} as its component arguments. Its notation #{f\gg g}
applies #{f} first, so its residual is #{A-BC^{-1}E}.
For finite coordinate index types,
\leanref{Matrix.fromBlocks_eq_of_invertible₂₂} gives the corresponding
matrix factorization over a commutative ring; its four arguments become
#{(A,B,E,C)}. These support the factorization, while the subsequent
[inverse](fdip-001V) and [kernel](fdip-001W)
statements have their own proofs.}
